A constant multi-base unit displays the way a var holding it does.

evalConversion folded `string(x)` for a single-base unit only: evalLiteral
declines to reduce a multi-base value to one number, so `"A {pad} B"` over a
`const pad measurement` stayed unfolded. The compiled targets spell it at run
time with their unit helper and were right; html's static page writes a
non-reactive text only when it folds to a literal, and wrote an empty span.
The fold now sums the per-base magnitudes of the literals, consts, sums and
differences a constant is written as, and displays them by the rule
`ir.FormatUnitTerm` states -- `mix` is 16px + 2em - 16px + 1rem, which is 18em.

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

const pad ui.measurement = 16px
const mix ui.measurement = pad + 2em - 16px + 1rem
const nothing ui.measurement = 3px - 3px

ui.window {
    var held ui.measurement = 16px

    ui.text(value="A {pad} B")
    ui.text(value="C {held} D")
    ui.text(value="E {mix} F")
    ui.text(value="G {nothing} H")
}
